// Copilot review (PR #2515), round-3 comment 4113834074:
// `checker_check_expr_call_inplace`'s `Type::method` associated-function
// arm (self-hosted/check/check.sio) resolves a 2-segment path's penultimate
// segment through the GLOBAL struct/enum tables via `fn_sig_table_find_
// method_semantic` before ever asking whether that segment is a module the
// CURRENT caller actually `use`d. `zz` here names both: the real module
// `zz.sio` (via `use zz;` below, exporting `f() -> i64 { 42 }`) AND this
// file's own unrelated lowercase `struct zz` with its own method `f`.
// Before the fix, `zz::f()` always bound the struct's method regardless of
// the real module import; the fix consults checker_use_module_known FIRST,
// so a qualifier the caller actually `use`d as a module wins over an
// incidentally-same-named global type.
//
// Checked with `--check` only, not compiled and run: this exact shape --
// a real module and an unrelated impl method sharing one qualifier --
// separately trips the STILL-OPEN lowerer-side gap this same review round
// raised (comment 4113834083, self-hosted/ir/lower.sio's
// callee_path_module_stripped_name): LOWER_IMPL_METHOD_HASH is global with
// no per-import provenance either, so codegen for this identical
// ambiguous call can still target the wrong body even once the checker
// accepts it correctly. See this fixture's own README for why closing
// that needs the shared "qualifier -> target-module-id" resolution table
// already documented elsewhere in this PR as a genuine architectural
// addition, not something fixable locally the way the checker's arm was.
use zz;

struct zz {
    n: i64,
}

impl zz {
    fn f(self) -> i64 with Mut, Panic, Div, Alloc {
        self.n + 1000
    }
}

fn main() -> i32 with IO, Mut, Panic {
    let v: i64 = zz::f()
    if v == 42 {
        println("MODULE_QUALIFIER_WINS_OVER_TYPE_COLLISION PASS")
        return 0
    }
    1
}
